# File I/O

File words return **ior** (0 = ok). For application code, pass ior to `throw` immediately — error code stays on TOS for quick consumption (see `forth-stack.mdc`).

## Open and assign

```forth
s" input.txt"  r/o open-file throw  Value fd-in
s" output.txt" w/o create-file throw Value fd-out
```

Modes: `r/o`, `r/w`, `w/o`. Wrap in helpers when reused:

```forth
0 Value fd-in
: open-input ( c-addr u -- )
  r/o open-file throw to fd-in ;
```

## Line buffer

```forth
256 Constant max-line
Create line-buf  max-line 2 + allot
```

`read-line ( c-addr u fd -- u2 flag ior )` — `flag` false at EOF.

## Read loop

```forth
: copy-lines ( -- )
  begin
    line-buf max-line fd-in read-line throw
  while
    line-buf swap fd-out write-line throw
  repeat
  drop ;
```

`while` leaves bytes-read on stack at EOF — clean up in the `else` / after branch.

## Close

```forth
fd-in  close-file throw
fd-out close-file throw
```

## Stack design

File words follow the ior-on-TOS convention: check errors before using other results. Do not ignore `ior` — see `forth-portability.mdc`.
